{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release, slice 2: the reader's page (design for council round 76)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: revision 2, for council round 76b. No code yet.** Revision 2 answers `codex-r76.md`:\n     4\t- source addresses are plain text, never links;\n     5\t- the escaping is written out as literal JSON escapes, with the charset and the CSP first in the file;\n     6\t- the format-1 checks are updated, and a lone page-hash error has its own test;\n     7\t- the screen check compares the expanded `v8` card with frozen expected entries;\n     8\t- the fetch mutant keeps its script running and must name `connect-src` and its destination;\n     9\t- the fallback, timer, phone-width and dark-scheme conditions are made observable;\n    10\t- the text filter is dropped. Slice 1 is built and accepted (D759: `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11,\n    11\t`RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`). Council 73 (D758) ordered the exporter first and the reader's screen second. This slice completes D756's\n    12\tscreen check: a $0 check of the reader's screen on a stored number question that has a forecast and history, where only the\n    13\tquestion, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear.\n    14\t\n    15\t## 0. Scope\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Built:**\n    18\t  - a page that is a pure function of the package data;\n    19\t  - the package gains that page (format 2);\n    20\t  - the package check proves the page equals that function of the data;\n    21\t  - a headless-browser check of the `old-1/v8` screen.\n    22\t- **The page reads only the package's data,** embedded in it.\n    23\t  - No network request of any kind: no live POC service, no `/library` fetch, no translated or prepared JSON, no composite trials,\n    24\t    no fonts or images from outside.\n    25\t  - No idea submission control or route (council 74).\n    26\t- **Not in scope:**\n    27\t  - the POC's page, API and MCP, unchanged;\n    28\t  - colleagues' page;\n    29\t  - the idea path;\n    30\t  - any model call;\n    31\t  - commit, publish, deploy (Alex's).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Why a separate page, not the POC's library UI.** The POC's components read the `thesis-ui/2` contract, which needs a ladder,\n    34\tscores and judgements for every thesis; a number question in the package has none. Adapting those shared components would change\n    35\tthe POC, which stays unchanged (design §2, D757). A small page written for the package has nothing to adapt and nothing that can\n    36\treach the network.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 1. What is built\n    39\t\n    40\t**A visible fallback.** The page starts with the line \"This release page needs JavaScript to show its questions.\" The script removes it\n    41\tonce the list is built. A browser that blocks the inline script keeps that line (`<noscript>` would stay hidden there; council\n    42\t76).\n    43\t\n    44\t**In `src/hivemind/release.py`:**\n    45\t- **`FORMAT = 'hivemind-release/2'`.** The data's allow-list (`release-allow-list/1`) is unchanged.\n    46\t- **`PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json', 'index.html'}`.** The manifest's closed set gains `page_sha256`.\n    47\t- **`render_page(data_bytes) -> bytes`:** one fixed template, the module constant `PAGE_TEMPLATE`, with the data inserted once.\n    48\t  - **The data** goes into `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"release-data\">`, written from the exact `release-data.json` bytes,\n    49\t    with each of these characters replaced by its six-character JSON escape: `<` by `\\u003c`, `>` by `\\u003e`, `&` by `\\u0026`,\n    50\t    U+2028 by `\\u2028`, U+2029 by `\\u2029`. These replacements stay valid JSON and parse back to the same data. The block can then\n    51\t    hold no `</script` and no `<!--`.\n    52\t  - **The file starts** with `<!doctype html>`, then `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the CSP `<meta>`, before any style, script or\n    53\t    data.\n    54\t  - **A Content-Security-Policy `<meta>`:**\n    55\t    `default-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-<inline script>'; style-src 'sha256-<inline style>'; img-src 'none'; font-src 'none';\n    56\t    connect-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'`. The two hashes are computed from the template's own script and style,\n    57\t    so the browser refuses any other script, style or connection.\n    58\t  - **The script** builds the page only with `document.createElement`, `textContent` and `className`. It creates no `<a>` element,\n    59\t    no `href` and no `src`. It never uses `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write`, `eval`, `Function`, or any network API\n    60\t    (`fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `EventSource`, `navigator.sendBeacon`, `import()`, `Worker`).\n    61\t  - **The style** uses system fonts and inline colour tokens copied from `web/design-system/tokens.css`. It has a dark scheme under\n    62\t    `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, and it works at phone width.\n    63\t- **`write_package`** also writes `index.html = render_page(data bytes)` and records its sha256.\n    64\t- **`check_package`** also:\n    65\t  - reads `index.html` once through the same folder handle;\n    66\t  - requires its bytes to equal `render_page(the data bytes it read)` exactly;\n    67\t  - requires the manifest's `page_sha256` to match.\n    68\t\n    69\t  A page that differs by one byte from the function of the data is refused. So a page cannot carry anything the data does not hold,\n    70\t  and no code the template does not hold.\n    71\t\n    72\t**What the page shows,** in package order. Each shown value sits in an element with a fixed class, so the screen check can read it:\n    73\t`question-text`, `question-status`, `question-forecast`, `question-answer`, `evidence-count`, `evidence-summary`, `evidence-quote`,\n    74\t`evidence-meta`, `source-title`, `source-publisher`, `source-day`, `source-address`.\n    75\t- **A header:** \"Hivemind release\", the package's day, and the count of questions by class.\n    76\t- **Each question:**\n    77\t  - its question text and \"Open\" or \"Closed\";\n    78\t  - for a number question, the marker \"Forecast unavailable\";\n    79\t  - for an event or Yes/No question, \"Answer: <label>\", or \"Not yet answered\" when the answer is null;\n    80\t  - \"<n> pieces of evidence\", and a `<details>` element. When opened, it builds the evidence list:\n    81\t    - each piece shows its summary, the quote, and a line with tier, speaker, and original speaker and day;\n    82\t    - each source shows title, publisher, day and its address as plain text. The page has no links, so neither a click nor a script\n    83\t      can make a request (council 76).\n    84\t\n    85\t  Evidence is built only when opened, so the first screen stays fast on a 6 MB package.\n    86\t\n    87\t## 2. State × event (the page and the check)\n    88\t\n    89\t| State / event | What happens |\n    90\t|---|---|\n    91\t| Package with no questions | \"No questions as of <day>\" |\n    92\t| A question with no evidence | \"No evidence in force\", and no `<details>` |\n    93\t| A source address of any scheme, `javascript:` included | shown as text; the page has no links |\n    94\t| Text holding `</script>`, `<img onerror=…>`, `&` or U+2028 | the embed escapes it, the page shows it as text, and the JSON parses back identical |\n    95\t| The page is opened from disk, a static host or any path | it reads only its embedded data; nothing else exists to read |\n    96\t| The browser blocks the inline script | the fallback line stays visible; nothing else renders, and the page holds no forecast to leak |\n    97\t| A page edited after the build (a fetch added, a field added) | `check_package` refuses: the bytes differ from `render_page(data)` |\n    98\t| The template changes in a later version | old packages fail `check_package` under the new code by design; a new template is a new format version |\n    99\t| A dark scheme and a phone width | checked in §6: at 390 px wide the page has no horizontal scroll; with the dark scheme emulated, the body's background is the dark token |\n   100\t\n   101\t## 3. Value × reader\n   102\t\n   103\t- Every value the page shows comes from the embedded data; the page reads no other value.\n   104\t- The page's readers are a person's browser and the browser check.\n   105\t- The data's readers are unchanged from slice 1: `check_package`, `check_origins`, the tests. The page adds one reader of the data,\n   106\t  the page script, and no new writer.\n   107\t\n   108\t## 4. New path × old checks\n   109\t\n   110\t| Slice-1 check | Slice 2 |\n   111\t|---|---|\n   112\t| Closed set of the data, origin check, staged check before the no-replace publication, the no-replace report | unchanged |\n   113\t| The manifest's closed set | gains `page_sha256`; `format` becomes `hivemind-release/2` |\n   114\t| Exactly the package files, each read once, no links, no pipes | the same rule over three files |\n   115\t| The literal-values rule of verification (council 75d) | applied to the page checks as well |\n   116\t| `release_check.py` pins `hivemind-release/1`; its helpers that forge data refresh only the data hash | the pin becomes `/2`. The forging helpers also regenerate `index.html = render_page(forged data)` and its hash, so a forged forecast still reaches the store comparison and is refused there, with its exact reason |\n   117\t\n   118\t## 5. Tests (`tests/test_release.py`) and mutants\n   119\t\n   120\t1. **Determinism.** `render_page` of the fixture data twice gives the same bytes.\n   121\t2. **Escaping.**\n   122\t   - A fixture quote holding `</script><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`, an `&` and a U+2028 is embedded with the literal escapes.\n   123\t   - The page bytes hold no `</script` inside the data block and no `<!--`.\n   124\t   - Parsing the block back gives the data unchanged.\n   125\t   - In the browser check (§6), on the fixture page with that evidence expanded, the exact quote is visible as text, and the page has\n   126\t     no `img` element.\n   127\t3. **No network code.**\n   128\t   - The template's script contains none of the listed APIs, and no `createElement('a')`, `href` or `src`.\n   129\t   - Its CSP is exactly the policy above, with the hashes of its own script and style.\n   130\t4. **The package check:**\n   131\t   - a page changed by one byte, with the manifest hash refreshed, is refused with its exact reason;\n   132\t   - a page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added, its CSP hash and manifest hash both updated, is refused, because it\n   133\t     is not `render_page(data)`;\n   134\t   - **a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong is refused with its exact reason**, which catches a deleted hash check;\n   135\t   - a missing page and a fourth file are each refused;\n   136\t   - all three files are read once through the folder handle, and the page bytes compared are the bytes hashed.\n   137\t5. **Literal identifiers:** `hivemind-release/2` and `release-allow-list/1`, in the tests and the acceptance script.\n   138\t\n   139\t**Mutants:**\n   140\t- `render_page` not escaping `<`;\n   141\t- the script using `innerHTML` for the quote;\n   142\t- a source address rendered as a link;\n   143\t- `check_package` not comparing the page with `render_page`;\n   144\t- `check_package` not checking `page_sha256`;\n   145\t- the CSP hash computed over a different script.\n   146\t\n   147\t## 6. The $0 screen check (`release_screen.mjs`, run once)\n   148\t\n   149\t- **Setup.** Headless Chrome from `/Applications/Google Chrome.app`, driven through its DevTools protocol with Node's built-in\n   150\t  WebSocket, on a fresh profile in the scratchpad. Each page is opened from `file://`.\n   151\t- **Packages:** `old-1` at its default day, and the two dated leak packages (`old-3` at 2026-08-25, `old-1` at 2026-08-28), built by\n   152\t  the slice-1 command under format 2.\n   153\t\n   154\t**Recorded for each page:**\n   155\t- the page's own network requests (`Network.requestWillBeSent` for the page target). Only the document itself may appear;\n   156\t- console errors, exceptions and CSP violations (`Runtime.exceptionThrown`, `Log.entryAdded`). Zero are allowed.\n   157\t\n   158\t**Observation.** The `Network`, `Log` and `Runtime` domains are enabled before navigation and stay enabled through every expansion.\n   159\tAny of these fails the page:\n   160\t- a request other than the document;\n   161\t- a `Log.entryAdded` error;\n   162\t- a `Runtime.exceptionThrown`;\n   163\t- a `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` of type `error`.\n   164\t\n   165\t**Expected values, frozen before the run.** `release_screen.mjs` reads `screen-expected.json`, written from the accepted slice-1\n   166\tpackage (`poc/data/release-check-2026-11-23g/old-1/package`) before the first screen run, with its sha256 recorded in the result. It\n   167\tholds:\n   168\t- `v8`'s question, \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\";\n   169\t- \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\" and \"1284 pieces of evidence\";\n   170\t- `v8`'s 1,284 evidence entries in order: summary, quote, meta line, and each source's title, publisher, day and address;\n   171\t- the two dated cards' day, question and answer \"Yes\".\n   172\t\n   173\t**The screen of `old-1/v8`:**\n   174\t- **Collapsed:** the card's `question-text`, `question-status`, `question-forecast` and `evidence-count` equal the frozen values. The\n   175\t  card has no `question-answer`.\n   176\t- **Expanded:** the displayed entries, read by class in order, equal the frozen 1,284 entries exactly, the full Morningstar quote and\n   177\t  its attribution included. A missing, duplicated or misplaced entry fails.\n   178\t- **Closed and reopened once:** the count of displayed entries is still 1,284.\n   179\t- **No link anywhere on the page:** zero `a` elements, zero `[href]`, zero `[src]`.\n   180\t- **Forbidden text:** the card's non-evidence text holds none of `v8`'s five labels, none of its five conditions, and not the\n   181\t  system's \"About $390 billion\" (`j1580`). The word \"Bullish\" may appear only where the frozen evidence holds it.\n   182\t- **A screenshot** of the expanded card's top is saved with the result.\n   183\t\n   184\t**The leak screens.** For `old-3/v36` at 2026-08-25 and `old-1/v10` at 2026-08-28:\n   185\t- the page's day and the card's question equal the frozen values, and `question-answer` is \"Answer: Yes\";\n   186\t- with the card expanded, the page text holds neither judgement's explanation nor its phrases \"around $1 trillion\", \"rung 4\" or\n   187\t  \"at rung\".\n   188\t\n   189\t**The escaping screen.** The fixture page of test 2, with that evidence expanded, shows the exact quote as text and has no `img`\n   190\telement.\n   191\t\n   192\t**The fetch mutant.**\n   193\t- **Setup:** a copy of the `old-1` page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added to its script, and the CSP script hash\n   194\t  recomputed for the altered script, so the script runs.\n   195\t- **It must fail the check for the right reason:**\n   196\t  - the normal list renders;\n   197\t  - a `Log.entryAdded` security error names `http://127.0.0.1:59999/library` and `connect-src`.\n   198\t\n   199\t  A page whose script is blocked wholesale does not earn the credit.\n   200\t- **Measured on this Mac (Chrome 158, headless, scratchpad probe):** a CSP-blocked fetch produces no `Network.requestWillBeSent`. It\n   201\t  produces a `security` error naming the address and the directive, and a `javascript` error.\n   202\t- **The probe page named port 8788.** Chrome refused it under the CSP before connecting (\"Refused to connect\"), so no request reached\n   203\t  that service. Port 59999 is not on the fetch specification's blocked-port list, and nothing listens there.\n   204\t\n   205\t**Phone width and dark scheme.**\n   206\t- With `Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride` at 390 × 844, `document.scrollingElement.scrollWidth` is at most 390.\n   207\t- With `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, the body's computed background is the dark token.\n   208\t\n   209\t**Budget,** set before the first control:\n   210\t- from `Page.navigate` until the full question list is in the DOM, at most 3 s for the `old-1` page in headless Chrome;\n   211\t- the page's size at most the data's bytes plus 64 KB.\n   212\t\n   213\t## 7. Prior decisions\n   214\t\n   215\tD726, D756, D757, D758, D759; council 74 (no idea input); council 75d (literal expected values); D690 (poc-v118 stays the product);\n   216\tD163 (English product text).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round76-brief.md && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r76.md'","aggregated_output":"# Council round 76: review the slice-2 design (the reader's page) before any code (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reviewer. Do not edit any file. The repository is /Users/lchingen/hivemind. Cite file:line.\n\n## What to review\n\n`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md`, the design for slice 2 of the \"Forecast unavailable\" release. Slice 1 is\nbuilt and accepted (D759 at the end of `docs/DECISIONS-log.md`; `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11; `RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`;\n`src/hivemind/release.py`, `scripts/release_build.py`, `tests/test_release.py`). D756 asks for a $0 check of the reader's screen on\na stored number question with a forecast and history: only the question, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear. D758\nordered the exporter first, the screen second. Council 74 deferred idea submission.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. **Leaks.** Can anything other than the package data reach the reader's screen, or any network request leave the page? Consider:\n   the CSP, inline script and style hashes, `file://` origin rules, links, `<details>`, `noscript`, the escaping of the embedded JSON.\n2. **The page = `render_page(data)` rule.** Is that the right structural check for the page? Is it sound with a read-once package? Is\n   the format bump to `hivemind-release/2` the right handling?\n3. **The screen check (§6).** Will CDP on headless Chrome show a CSP-blocked fetch as a request or a violation? Is the v8 screen\n   assertion set right, and literal enough (council 75d's rule)? Is the budget set before the control?\n4. **The tables (§2–§4) and tests (§5).** Is any cell blank or wrong? Is any test unable to fail without its code?\n5. **Scope.** Is anything in this design scope drift beyond D756/D758, or a change to the POC?\n6. **Verdict:** ready to build, or not ready with each serious finding.\n\n**Out of scope:**\n- the frozen judging, opening and extraction pieces;\n- the closed v13s line;\n- slice 1's accepted internals, unless slice 2 breaks them;\n- the idea path;\n- the recorded evidence-summary disagreement;\n- wording.\n\nReport only findings that change what is built or whether it is safe.\n     1\t**Verdict: not ready to build.** The package check is sound, but the screen check could pass a page that says “1284 pieces of evidence” and shows only one summary. It could also reject a correct page because five genuine source titles contain “Bullish.” [RELEASE-PAGE.md:134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:134) · [RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:145](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:145)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **An ordinary source link breaks the zero-request promise.** I counted 1,258 HTTP(S) source entries under `old-1/v8`. Clicking one can navigate from the local file to the publisher. `noopener noreferrer` suppresses opener and referrer information. It still permits navigation. Opening `<details>` itself needs no request. For the promise written in §0, render the source address as plain text and check that expanding the evidence creates no navigable links. The `file://` test can then run with Chrome’s normal file restrictions. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:16) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:64) · [CSP navigation directives](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/#directives-navigation)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **The specified JSON escaping leaves the closing tag intact.** I checked the file bytes. Line 38 gives the same literal characters on both sides of “escaped as.” A source containing `</script>` can therefore end the data block before the page script reads it. Specify the literal JSON escape sequences `\\u003c`, `\\u003e`, `\\u0026`, `\\u2028` and `\\u2029`. Declare UTF-8 at the start of the file and put the CSP before the style, script and embedded data. Test the existing fixture in the browser with its evidence expanded. Its exact quote must be visible as text, with no image element created. The proposed “no second `<script>` start” check misses the closing tag, and parsing JSON alone never exercises the proposed `innerHTML` mutant. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:37) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:104](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:104) · [HTML script parsing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#restrictions-for-contents-of-script-elements) · [CSP meta placement](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/#meta-element)\n     6\t\n     7\t3. **Keep byte-for-byte rendering and format 2. Update the checks that depend on format 1.** Read all three files once through the open directory handle. Hash and compare those same page bytes against `render_page(raw_data)` using the same data bytes that passed the store check. That certifies the inspected copy. Adding a required file and manifest member justifies `/2` while the data allow-list stays `/1`. The existing acceptance script pins `/1`, and two helpers that deliberately alter data refresh only the data hash. Their format-2 copies must also regenerate the page and its hash so the forged forecast still reaches the store comparison. Add the converse case too: a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong must fail for that exact reason. Every proposed page-tampering test refreshes that hash, so deleting the hash check currently escapes them. [release.py:454](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/release.py:454) · [release_check.py:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:24) · [release_check.py:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:128) · [test_release.py:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_release.py:180) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:109](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:109)\n     8\t\n     9\t4. **Check what the reader sees both before and after expanding `v8`.** Pin the exact question, “How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?”, alongside the three stated labels. For the expanded card, freeze the expected ordered evidence and source values from the accepted package before the run. Compare the displayed entries with that expectation, including the full Morningstar quote and attribution. This catches missing, duplicated or misplaced entries and permits “Bullish” exactly where the source used it. Close and reopen the card once to catch a second copy being appended. Apply the same discipline to the two dated cards: pin the day and question, require “Answer: Yes,” expand their evidence, and check the known explanation phrases there too. A collapsed page-text search cannot inspect content the script has yet to create. [FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:134) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:69) · [release_check.py:173](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:173) · [RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:60)\n    10\t\n    11\t5. **Credit the fetch test only when Chrome identifies the attempted connection.** The Chrome 158 probe recorded in the design answers the CDP question for this Mac: the blocked fetch produced two log errors and zero `Network.requestWillBeSent` events. Recompute the altered inline script’s CSP hash so the test script actually runs. Require the normal card to render and the security event to name the intended destination and `connect-src`. A stale script hash would otherwise earn credit for blocking the whole script. Port 9 also has an independent browser block, so a generic fetch error there proves too little. Enable observation before navigation and keep it active through expansion. If “zero console errors” remains a condition, include `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` for `console.error`. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:130) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:144) · [Fetch port blocking](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#port-blocking) · [CDP console events](https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/tot/Runtime/#event-consoleAPICalled)\n    12\t\n    13\t6. **Correct two browser acceptance conditions.** A blocked inline script leaves browser scripting enabled, so `<noscript>` stays hidden. If the reader should see a fallback in that case, put ordinary visible text in the page and replace it after successful rendering. The 3-second and size budgets **are** set before the first screen control. Start the timer at navigation and stop when the full expected list is visible. Starting at `load` can omit the work of parsing and rendering the embedded data. The state table also promises a phone-width and dark-scheme check that §6 never performs. Either define its viewport and observable pass condition or remove the acceptance claim. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:79](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:79) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:155](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:155) · [HTML `noscript` behavior](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#the-noscript-element)\n    14\t\n    15\tThe separate release page fits D758 and requires no POC change. The text filter is optional for D756. I would leave it out of this slice so acceptance covers the reader’s actual task: opening a question and seeing all its sources with the forecast withheld. This was a read-only review. The Chrome observation above comes from the probe recorded in the design. [D756:11833](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11833) · [D758:11881](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11881) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:59](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:59)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
